{"id":1045,"date":"2026-03-23T07:28:57","date_gmt":"2026-03-23T07:28:57","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:10005\/?p=1045"},"modified":"2026-08-30T10:50:40","modified_gmt":"2026-03-23T09:26:26","slug":"%e5%9f%ba%e4%ba%8e%e5%8f%af%e5%8f%98%e5%b8%83%e9%9a%86%e8%bf%87%e6%bb%a4%e5%99%a8%e7%9a%84%e7%89%a9%e7%90%86%e5%b1%82%e5%ae%89%e5%85%a8%e4%bc%a0%e8%be%93%e5%a2%9e%e5%bc%ba%e6%97%a0%e7%ba%bf%e9%80%9a","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/en\/%e5%9f%ba%e4%ba%8e%e5%8f%af%e5%8f%98%e5%b8%83%e9%9a%86%e8%bf%87%e6%bb%a4%e5%99%a8%e7%9a%84%e7%89%a9%e7%90%86%e5%b1%82%e5%ae%89%e5%85%a8%e4%bc%a0%e8%be%93%e5%a2%9e%e5%bc%ba%e6%97%a0%e7%ba%bf%e9%80%9a\/","title":{"rendered":"Enhancing Wireless Communication Security via Physical Layer Secure Transmission Based on Variable Bloom Filters"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3749991f wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"min-height:1000px\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-458a08b5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\"><h2 class=\"lab-paper-title wp-block-post-title\">Enhancing Wireless Communication Security via Physical Layer Secure Transmission Based on Variable Bloom Filters<\/h2>\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">HUO A, LI G, YANG L, et al.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><em>IEEE Internet of Things Journal<\/em><\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-text-color has-contrast-3-color has-alpha-channel-opacity has-contrast-3-background-color has-background is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-46005010 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udccc Abstract<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote has-text-align-left lab-abstract-quote has-small-font-size\" style=\"margin-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);margin-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);padding-top:0;padding-right:0;padding-bottom:0;padding-left:0\"><blockquote><p>This paper investigates a scheme that leverages unpredictable physical layer channel characteristics to achieve one-time pad (OTP) secure communication. Existing OTP schemes based on physical layer secret key generation require an information reconciliation step, which introduces additional transmission overhead and faces security threats of information leakage under slowly varying channels. To address these challenges, this paper studies a fault-tolerant privacy amplification method based on variable Bloom filters. Specifically, the quantized bit sequence of channel state information is processed through BF to enhance sequence randomness, while BF parameters are dynamically adjusted to eliminate correlation between adjacent sequences. Furthermore, this paper optimizes error correction code parameters based on the eavesdropper's location and the quantized bit length, further enhancing system security. Comprehensive simulation results demonstrate that the proposed method achieves near-perfect pass rates in NIST randomness tests, with a bit replacement rate of approximately 0.45, while resisting attacks under slowly varying channels, significantly outperforming existing OTP secure transmission schemes.<\/p><\/blockquote><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-6adec98e lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udca1 Core Contributions<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<ul start=\"1\" style=\"border-style:none;border-width:0px\" class=\"wp-block-list lab-feature-list\">\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Propose the VBF-PST scheme to fundamentally address the issues of insufficient randomness and BRR in OTP secure transmission under quasi-static environments.<\/strong>: Process the quantized bit sequence using a variable BF, applying different hash functions to each round of sequences through time-varying parameters <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">P(T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>P(T). Even when input sequences exhibit strong correlation across adjacent time slots, the randomness and BRR of the output sequences can be maintained within a stable range; meanwhile, the information reconciliation step is eliminated, reducing communication overhead and lowering the risk of information leakage.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Design an adaptive ECC parameter optimization algorithm to enhance resilience against proximal attacks.<\/strong>: Based on the position of the eavesdropper Eve (i.e., channel error rate) and the quantized bit length, automatically compute the optimal error correction code parameters <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>t, ensuring that legitimate communicating parties Alice and Bob can correctly recover confidential information while Eve cannot decode it; both theoretical analysis and simulations jointly validate the scheme's effectiveness in resisting differential attacks and proximal attacks.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Derive theoretical guidelines for optimal parameter selection of VBF.<\/strong>: By computing the information entropy of the output bit sequence, derive the parameter combination that maximizes randomness, proving that when <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">K=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> or <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">K=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\u3001<math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>SET<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\text{SET}=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> , the highest entropy value is achieved, and theoretically explain the mechanism by which BF parameters affect randomness, providing clear parameter design guidelines for engineering implementation.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-2f9d57f4 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udd27 Method Overview<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-b46d2053 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-24-56.png ,http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-24-56.png 780w, http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-24-56.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-24-56.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1046\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_15-24-56\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">The key generation process of the VBF-PST scheme consists of four steps:<strong>Channel probing<\/strong>phase, Alice and Bob alternately transmit probing signals in TDD mode to obtain reciprocal channel measurements;<strong>Quantization<\/strong>phase, both parties perform equiprobable two-bit quantization on the channel measurements, yielding partially inconsistent quantized bit sequences <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo separator=\"true\">,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">q_{a,b}(T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\uff1b<strong>BF mapping<\/strong>phase, the quantized sequence undergoes VBF processing\u2014Alice and Bob share the same hash table <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>(containing <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> hash functions) and time-varying parameters <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">P(T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>, and the quantized sequence is mapped to an output sequence with higher randomness via the SET method <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo separator=\"true\">,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">q^{BF}_{a,b}(T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\uff1b<strong>Data transmission<\/strong>phase, Bob uses <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo separator=\"true\">,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">q^{BF}_{b,a}(T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> as the key to XOR-encrypt confidential information and transmits it, while Alice uses <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo separator=\"true\">,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">q^{BF}_{a,b}(T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> to decrypt and employs FEC to correct minor errors introduced by incomplete key agreement, thereby completing secure OTP transmission without information reconciliation.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-dda34f26 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcca Experimental Results<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-080de2b0 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-27-09-1024x428.png ,http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-27-09.png 780w, http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-27-09.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-27-09-1024x428.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1049\" width=\"1000\" height=\"400\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_15-27-09\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><figcaption class=\"uagb-image-caption\">Fig.9<\/figcaption><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-82ea6282 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-26-17-1024x431.png ,http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-26-17.png 780w, http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-26-17.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-26-17-1024x431.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1047\" width=\"1002\" height=\"400\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_15-26-17\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><figcaption class=\"uagb-image-caption\">Fig.10<\/figcaption><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-15bdad6d wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-26-48.png ,http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-26-48.png 780w, http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-26-48.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-26-48.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1048\" width=\"500\" height=\"500\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_15-26-48\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><figcaption class=\"uagb-image-caption\">Fig.11<\/figcaption><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-container-content-432d1f4f wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>The VBF-PST scheme improves the NIST randomness test pass rate from 0.1 for UK-PST to nearly 1 (approximately 99%), while maintaining the adjacent-frame bit replacement rate (BRR) stably around 0.45.<\/strong>: As shown in <strong>As shown in Fig. 9(a)(b)\u200b<\/strong> , under the parameter <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">K=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\u3001<math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>SET<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\text{SET}=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> configuration, the NIST test pass rates of VBF-PST at adjacent time instants <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">T_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\u200b and <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">T_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> are both close to 1, whereas UK-PST achieves only 0.1 (i.e., only 1 out of every 10 secure transmissions passes all NIST tests);<strong>Fig. 10<\/strong> further results show that under this configuration, the BRR remains stable around 0.45 across different Doppler frequencies (0\u20135 Hz), significantly outperforming UK-PST and effectively resisting differential attacks. Additionally,<strong>Fig. 11<\/strong> presents security analysis results demonstrating that through adaptive ECC parameter optimization, the key recovery success rate of legitimate users remains at a high level, while Eve's success rate stays consistently below 0.2.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-e3c2d50d lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcdd Citation<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<pre class=\"wp-block-code lab-bibtex-code has-small-font-size\"><code>@article{huo2025vbfpst,\n  author    = {Anqi Huo and Guyue Li and Lilin Yang and Zilong Liu and Aiqun Hu},\n  title     = {Enhancing Wireless Communication Security With Variable Bloom Filter-Based Physical-Layer Secure Transmission},\n  journal   = {IEEE Internet of Things Journal},\n  volume    = {12},\n  number    = {14},\n  pages     = {28835--28849},\n  month     = jul,\n  year      = {2025},\n  doi       = {10.1109\/JIOT.2025.3567288}\n}\n<\/code><\/pre>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div style=\"height:100px\" aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-spacer\"><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-content-justification-space-between is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-788841da wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>This paper investigates a scheme that leverages unpredictable physical layer channel characteristics to achieve one-time pad (OTP) secure communication. Existing OTP schemes based on physical layer secret key generation require an information reconciliation step, which introduces additional transmission overhead and faces security threats of information leakage under slowly varying channels. To address these challenges, this paper studies a fault-tolerant privacy amplification method based on variable Bloom filters. Specifically, the quantized bit sequence of channel state information is processed through BF to enhance sequence randomness, while BF parameters are dynamically adjusted to eliminate correlation between adjacent sequences. Furthermore, this paper optimizes error correction code parameters based on the eavesdropper's location and the quantized bit length, further enhancing system security. Comprehensive simulation results demonstrate that the proposed method achieves near-perfect pass rates in NIST randomness tests, with a bit replacement rate of approximately 0.45, while resisting attacks under slowly varying channels, significantly outperforming existing OTP secure transmission schemes.<\/p>","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":1046,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"mypost","format":"standard","meta":{"_uag_custom_page_level_css":"","footnotes":""},"categories":[5],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-1045","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-research"],"uagb_featured_image_src":{"full":["http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-24-56.png",831,442,false],"thumbnail":["http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-24-56-150x150.png",150,150,true],"medium":["http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-24-56-300x160.png",300,160,true],"medium_large":["http:\/\/jiushihema.github.io/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_15-24-56-768x408.png",768,408,true],"large":["http:\/\